{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">St James the Great in Church Street is Harvington's parish church, and its green spire is a landmark across the village. It is the H in the ARCH Benefice, the home of the benefice's Wednesday Eucharist, and is listed Grade I.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>The church through the centuries</h2>
    <p>The oldest parts of St James are Norman: the lower stages of the tower and the doorway from the tower into the nave were built in the 12th century, and lines on the west wall hint that a Saxon church stood here before. In the 14th century the nave was made wider and new windows were put in.</p>
    <p>The church took its present look in 1855, when Frederick Preedy carried out a restoration costing about £1,750. He gave the tower its spire and clock. The spire was covered in oak shingles at first; the copper that replaced them in 1947 has turned the green that Harvington knows today.</p>
    <h2>Things to see</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The font, a plain tub of stone carved in the 12th century.</li>
      <li>The east window, designed by Preedy, restored in 2008 and part of the Evesham Preedy trail.</li>
      <li>A wall memorial in the chancel to Thomas Feriman, who died in 1619.</li>
      <li>The bells: six, recast by John Taylor &amp; Co in 1855 and rehung in 1998 so they can be rung full circle.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
{%- set servicesIntro -%}<p>There is a service at St James on most Sundays, plus the Eucharist every Wednesday. Coming up:</p>{%- endset -%}
